  message: "PRIORITY CHANGED 2026-08-17, on owner authorisation, as part of the P0/P1 sweep. Recording the reasoning here because a re-rank that lives only in conversation is indistinguishable next session from drift.\n\nTHE GOVERNING MEASUREMENT. docs/measurements/0001-taint-flow-precision.md puts taint-flow precision at ~41% population-weighted across six real repositories. Per WI-sivuz's PRE-REGISTERED decision bands -- named before any interval was computed, so the interval cannot be used to move them -- that is the <50% band: \"precision is the headline problem and recall work should stop until it is understood.\"\n\nP1 -> P2. Same family -- emitting the missing kotlin/javascript instance-method call edges is a recall fix. Re-verified at tip 2026-08-17: kotlin emits nothing for fh.readText() and javascript nothing for obj.write(x), while sibling call shapes in the same file do emit."  # 49f3
- op: discuss  # defd
  at: '2026-08-23T17:48:37Z'  # defd
  by: agent  # defd
  actor: jgstern_agent  # defd
  clock: 8  # defd
  nonce: defd  # defd
  message: "THIS ITEM IS BIGGER THAN FILED: RUST BELONGS IN ITS TABLE. Measured 2026-08-23\non an authored fixture, ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/rust_fidelity_probe_08232026/,\nwhile probing a different question (whether verify-claims verdicts depend on\nanalyzer fidelity).\n\nTHE SHAPE, chosen per this item's own methodology to mirror a sink the\ncatalogue actually declares rather than an invented one -- io_primitives/\nrust.yaml declares net_send: std::net::UdpSocket methods [send, send_to]:\n\n    pub fn exfiltrate(sock: &UdpSocket, payload: &[u8]) -> std::io::Result<usize> {\n        sock.send(payload)\n    }\n\nEMITTED: NO CALL EDGE AT ALL. The enclosing function's only outbound edge is a\nmodule_attr_ref to std.io from its RETURN TYPE annotation. No `send` appears\nanywhere in the run's edge set. Identical in both arms (tree-sitter and\nrust-analyzer/SCIP).\n\nPOSITIVE CONTROL IN THE SAME RUN, so the null is evidence and not a broken\nharness: `fs::read_to_string(path)` in the same file emits\n  calls -> rust:std::fs:0-0:read_to_string:external_symbol\nand the fs_read claim correctly returns VIOLATED with 1 chain.\n\nWHY THIS IS WORSE THAN THE KOTLIN/JAVASCRIPT CELLS THIS ITEM ALREADY HOLDS.\nIn Rust the receiver's type is named BY CONSTRUCTION -- parameters are always\nannotated, `sock: &UdpSocket` is right there in the signature, and UdpSocket is\ncatalogued. There is no type-inference gap to blame. The analyzer has the type\nin hand and emits nothing.\n\nAND THE GENERIC SHAPE FARES BETTER THAN THE ANNOTATED ONE, which is the tell\nthat this is an emission gap rather than a resolution one. In the same file:\n\n    pub fn drain<W: Write>(w: &mut W, payload: &[u8]) -> std::io::Result<()> {\n        w.write_all(payload)\n    }\n\nDOES emit, as rust:external:0-0:write_all:external_symbol -- untyped, but\npresent and therefore disclosable. So the case where the type IS syntactically\navailable produces nothing, while the case where it is NOT produces an edge.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE VERDICT. A net_send claim over this crate cannot be\nviolated, because the edge that would carry the violation does not exist. The\nrun returns inconclusive, but for unrelated reasons (INV-pusin / INV-rilit),\nnot because anything noticed the egress.\n\nNOT MEASURED: the two-step vs chained distinction this item's table draws, for\nrust specifically -- only the two-step form was exercised. Whether any rust\ninstance-method call on an external type emits, or only the generic-parameter\nform. Whether the java/scala/swift cells still hold."  # defd
